insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 1, 'Page OT30020 of HM Revenue and Customs'' Oil Taxation Manual (Open Government Licence v3.0, updated 8 August 2019) describes a Farm Out in one sentence. What trade does that sentence describe?', '["A sale of a share in a producing field, which the manual says is generally paid for in cash or in shares.", "An undertaking to bear its own and the outgoing party''s share of exploration or appraisal costs.", "The owner, the Farmer Out, disposing of a licence interest for consideration given by the Farmer In.", "An agreement that completes the whole work obligation before any share is assigned."]'::jsonb, 2, 'OT30020 describes a Farm Out as the disposal of a licence interest by the owner, the Farmer Out, in return for consideration given by the Farmer In. Cash or shares is how OT30023 describes the price of a share in a producing field. Bearing both parties'' share of the costs is how OT30048 describes an obligation to undertake work. Work completed before the assignment is what OT30021 calls an earn-in.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 2, 'Where does the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 (Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021) define a farm-out?', '["In s.94(8)(b), which sits inside the Act''s section on marginal fields.", "In s.95(14), the subsection that also defines a change of control of a licence holder by its voting power.", "In s.233(10), where the Act requires a decommissioning and abandonment plan in every farm out agreement.", "Nowhere in the Act itself: the definition is reg. 24 of the 2024 Assignment of Interests Regulations."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Act defines a farm-out in s.94(8)(b), and s.94(8) opens "For the purpose of this section" inside the marginal field section. Section 95(14) defines a change of control, s.233(10) uses the word farm out without a second definition, and reg. 24 of the 2024 Regulations defines a petroleum agreement and an assignment. The engine''s arithmetic applies to any farm-out a caller states.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 3, 'In the Ekene Deep fixture, which party is the farmor, and what participating interest does it hold before the farm-out?', '["PA, Partner A (synthetic), which holds 30.000000 percent and stays outside the trade.", "EKO, Ekene Operator (synthetic), with 70.000000 percent.", "FIN, Farminee Energy (synthetic), which holds 0.000000 percent until it earns its share.", "EKO and PA jointly, as co-farmors of the licence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture names EKO the farmor and FIN the farminee, with EKO at 70.000000 percent, PA at 30.000000 and FIN at 0.000000 before the deal. PA is a licence party outside the trade: it pays its own share and keeps its participating interest. FIN is the newcomer that earns. No call has two farmors; the engine reads one farmor id.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 4, 'On what date was each text in the course''s sources table read?', '["27 August 2021, which is the gazette date printed against the Petroleum Industry Act.", "9 April 2024, the gazette date of the Assignment of Interests Regulations, 2024.", "13 March 2024, the date on which the 2024 Assignment of Interests Regulations were made.", "2026-09-27, the same date for all four texts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every text in the sources table, the Act, the 2024 Regulations, HMRC''s manual pages and the Penn State page, was read on 2026-09-27, and the table prints that date against each one. 27 August 2021 is the Act''s gazette date, 9 April 2024 the Regulations'' gazette date and 13 March 2024 the date they were made: each is an edition date, and none is the date the course read a text.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 5, 'How does the course give the edition of the Nigerian Upstream Petroleum (Assignment of Interests) Regulations, 2024?', '["S.I. No. 61 of 2024, Official Gazette No. 67, Vol. 111, 9 April 2024 (made 13 March 2024), as the cover lists it.", "S.I. No. 67 of 2024, Official Gazette No. 61, Vol. 111, 9 April 2024 (made 13 March 2024).", "Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021, the gazette that also carries the Act itself.", "S.I. No. 67 of 2024, Gazette No. 61, Vol. 111, 13 March 2024 (made 9 April 2024)."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sources table gives S.I. No. 67 of 2024, Official Gazette No. 61, Vol. 111, 9 April 2024, made 13 March 2024. Swapping 67 and 61 names a different instrument and gazette, and swapping the two dates puts the gazette before the making. Official Gazette No. 142, Vol. 108, 27 August 2021 is the edition of the Petroleum Industry Act 2021.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 6, 'The course cites the Penn State EME 801 page, Lesson 6, for its printed figures alone. Why does it use none of the page''s sentences?', '["Its CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 licence is non-commercial, and this course is sold.", "Its figures are the only public farm-in schedule in print.", "It is a licensed model agreement, taught by concept only.", "Its sentences describe United Kingdom tax rules, which the course reads and applies to none of its deals."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sources table records the page''s licence as CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, which is non-commercial, and this course is sold, so its payoffs and EMVs are cited as figures with the citation and no sentence is quoted. No public text prints a farm-in schedule, as the engine''s validation record says. The licensed forms are the model agreements, and the United Kingdom guidance is HMRC''s manual.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 7, 'A lesson needs the idea of a promote, which licensed model farm-out agreements carry. How does the course treat those licensed forms?', '["It quotes the relevant clause once with a full citation, since a citation makes a short quotation fair.", "It quotes each form only in the lesson that first needs the clause, then refers to the clause number.", "It teaches the idea from public texts and the engine''s stated arithmetic, and neither quotes a form nor names one as a source.", "It paraphrases each clause line by line and keeps the form''s own numbering, so a learner can check it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sources section says no licensed model agreement is quoted anywhere in the course and none is named as a source; where a lesson needs an idea such agreements carry, it teaches the idea from the public texts and the engine''s stated arithmetic. A citation does not license a quotation, and a line-by-line paraphrase keyed to the form''s numbering would reproduce the form.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 8, 'In the earning calculator''s box for the Ekene Deep well, a learner changes EKO''s participatingPct from 70 to 60 and leaves PA at 30. What does the engine return?', '["A result leaving the missing ten points unassigned while FIN still earns 30 percent.", "A result in which EKO and PA are scaled up in proportion until they sum to 100, with a reason line reporting the change.", "A refusal on events[0].earnedPct, since 30 percent is half of the farmor''s 60.", "A refusal on the field parties, saying the participating interests must sum to 100 and got a sum of 90."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine checks that the parties'' participating interests make up the whole, and refuses otherwise, in its own words: "parties must have participatingPct summing to 100; got a sum of 90". It never rescales or fills a gap, since every share is a stated input. An earning of 30 from a farmor at 60 is within the farmor''s interest, so the earning check would pass.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 9, 'A learner adds a top-level key carryCap to an earning box that is otherwise valid. What does the engine do with it?', '["The call runs, and carryCap is read as a carry-amount cap on the first event of the box, at the amount stated.", "It refuses the call on carryCap and lists the eight top-level keys the function reads.", "It runs the call and quietly drops carryCap as a key it does not read.", "It refuses on events[0].cap, saying a cap must be stated inside the event it applies to."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every function refuses an input key it does not read, at every level, naming the key and the accepted keys. In the engine''s words: "carryCap is not an accepted key; the accepted keys at the top level are parties, farmor, farminee, events, vesting, eventsCompleted, cashBonus, pastCosts". A misspelt optional key is refused so that a term is never silently dropped, and a cap belongs inside an event''s cap object. The refusal names carryCap itself, at the top level, and no event field.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 10, 'A box has no vesting rule and also carries an unknown top-level key vest. Which problem does the engine name?', '["The unknown key vest: every function checks its accepted keys before it reads a term.", "The missing vesting rule, since a missing required term is checked ahead of any extra key in the box.", "Both problems in one message, listing the missing vesting rule first and the unknown key vest second.", "Neither of them: vest is read as the vesting rule, and the call returns a result with a reason line."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course''s order of refusals says a box that carries an unknown key and lacks a required term is refused on the unknown key first. On the Ekene single well with vesting removed and a key vest added, the engine names vest and lists the accepted top-level keys. One refusal names one field, and the engine never reads a key it does not know as another term.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 11, 'A box for the Ekene licence (EKO 70 and PA 30 percent) names the farminee''s id as PA. What does the engine do?', '["It computes a farm-in between partners, moving 30 percent of the licence from EKO across to PA''s holding.", "It merges PA''s positions and reports PA at 60 percent after the deal.", "It refuses on farminee.id: the farminee must have an id no licence party has.", "It refuses on farmor, since EKO may not farm out to a party already on its licence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses a farminee that is already a licence party. Its own words: farminee.id must be an id no licence party has (EKO, PA); got "PA". A trade between existing partners is a different trade, and the earning call does not compute it. The engine never merges roles, and the farmor EKO is a valid stated input, so the refusal names the farminee field alone.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 12, 'Which figures does the farmout engine hold for itself, with no caller stating them?', '["A standard promote of 10 points, applied when a deal leaves the share paid out.", "Zero for the cash bonus and the reimbursement of any deal that states neither.", "Per-event vesting, written in for any deal that states only one event.", "Size caps and a sum tolerance in DEFAULTS, and the cited gazetted figures in NIGERIA_ASSIGNMENT."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The stated constants are the exported DEFAULTS (the most parties, events, years and so on, and SUM_TOLERANCE of 1e-9) and NIGERIA_ASSIGNMENT, whose figures are each cited to the 2024 Regulations or the Act. Every deal term (the share paid, the interest earned, the cap, the vesting rule, the bonus, the reimbursement) is an input with no default, and a call without one is refused by name.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 13, 'What does the Ekene farm-out fixture file say about its own data?', '["It is a redacted copy of a real Nigerian farm-out, with the parties'' names replaced by Ekene names.", "It is SYNTHETIC teaching data, with no real company, deal, prospect, price or regulator decision.", "It reproduces the Penn State drill or farm out problem under Ekene names.", "It is the regulator''s worked example from the 2024 Regulations, converted from naira to US dollars."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture labels itself: "SYNTHETIC teaching data for the Ekene field (ours). No real company, deal, prospect, price or regulator decision. The Ekene Deep prospect and every party below are synthetic." It is written by a stated script, every party in it is synthetic, and no public text the course reads prints a farm-in schedule.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 14, 'Where does an Associate learner run the practicals of this course?', '["In the earning calculator, the course''s own panel, calling the vendored engine the lessons quote.", "In a Suite farm-out app, opened from the economics module on the dashboard.", "In a spreadsheet the learner builds from the lesson tables.", "In the valuation calculator, which serves every tier from one view."]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is an engine course with no Suite app. Each tier has its own calculator panel calling the same vendored engine: the earning calculator at Associate, the deal calculator at Professional and the valuation calculator at Expert. Every graded number is a return value of the engine on fixed inputs, which no hand-built spreadsheet stands in for.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-farm-out-is', 15, 'In its own sentences, what does the course call the party that gives up part of its participating interest?', '["The farmee, the Act''s word in s.94(5).", "The Farmer Out, as HMRC''s manual prints it on page OT30020.", "The farmor, the engine''s name; each text''s own word stays inside its quotation marks.", "The assignor, the word the 2024 Regulations use for the party that pays the assignment consent fee."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The vocabulary rule makes farmor and farminee the engine''s names for the party giving up an interest and the party earning it, with the texts'' words quoted as they print them. "farmee" in the Act is the incoming party. Farmer Out and assignor are the texts'' words for the outgoing party; the course quotes them and writes farmor in its own sentences.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 1, 'The Ekene Deep-1 exploration well: EKO holds 70 and PA 30 percent; FIN pays 40 percent of a gross cost of 46000000.000000 to earn 30 percent from EKO; the event carries a cap on the gross cost of 44000000.000000 with the overrun rule "post-deal-interests", and it is completed. What does the engine return as FIN''s payment for the well?', '["16000000.000000, the stated 40 percent applied to the dry-hole cost of 40000000.000000.", "13800000.000000, a heads-up 30 percent of the well that matches the participating interest FIN earns.", "14000000.000000, which the engine''s reason line prints beside FIN''s name as its part of the well.", "18200000.000000, which is 39.565217 percent of the gross cost."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s event row prints FIN paying 18200000.000000, 39.565217 percent of the 46000000.000000 well, and its reason line says the promote applies to 44000000 with the excess paid by the post-deal interests. 16000000.000000 would use the dry-hole cost, which is no term of the earning event. 13800000.000000 is PA''s payment, a plain 30 percent of the well. 14000000.000000 is what EKO pays.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 2, 'On the Ekene Deep-1 row the engine prints FIN paying 18200000.000000, EKO 14000000.000000 and PA 13800000.000000. What check does the course make with those three payments?', '["Their total is 44000000.000000, the cap, with the excess billed to the parties later.", "Their total is 46000000.000000, the gross cost of the well, so the whole well is paid.", "FIN''s payment equals EKO''s and PA''s together, the farminee paying for both.", "EKO''s payment equals PA''s, since both licence parties hold what the farminee leaves after its promote."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course adds the three and finds the gross cost: 18200000.000000 + 14000000.000000 + 13800000.000000 = 46000000.000000 (derived), the whole well. The excess above the cap is paid inside the same split, so the total is the well and no later bill appears. FIN pays only the farmor''s side of the well and none of PA''s, and EKO''s 14000000.000000 differs from PA''s 13800000.000000.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 3, 'Which function does the farmout engine import to split each gross cost among the parties outside the trade?', '["calculatePartnerCosts, from engines/economics/afe.js.", "applyJV, from engines/economics/cashflow.ts, which the engine imports for its working-interest scaling.", "carryRecovery, from engines/economics/jointVenture.js, which runs the carry ledger after a farm-in.", "portfolioRiskMetrics, from engines/economics/portfolio.js, the seeded Monte Carlo of the engine."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s basis says the other parties pay their own interests of each gross cost through calculatePartnerCosts from engines/economics/afe.js, imported and reused. applyJV scales a success-case value by a working interest, carryRecovery runs a development carry after the farm-in, and portfolioRiskMetrics is the one sampling routine; none of them splits a well among the parties.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 4, 'PA holds 30 percent of the Ekene licence and takes no part in the farm-out between EKO and FIN. On the completed Ekene Deep-1 well, with a gross cost of 46000000.000000, what does the engine return as PA''s payment?', '["0.000000, since the farminee carries PA.", "12000000.000000, its 30 percent of the dry-hole cost of 40000000.000000 in the fixture.", "13800000.000000, its own 30 percent of the gross cost.", "4400000.000000, its share of the carry that the farminee pays across the whole licence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The other parties pay their own interests of each gross cost, so PA pays 30 percent of 46000000.000000, which the engine returns as 13800000.000000. The farminee pays part of the farmor''s share only, so PA is carried by nobody. The dry-hole cost is no term of the earning event, and the carry of 4400000.000000 is the part of EKO''s share that FIN pays.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 5, 'Drilled and completed: a 40000000.000000 well, uncapped, in which FIN takes 30 percent of EKO''s 70 by paying 40 percent (PA keeps 30). What is the farmor''s own payment?', '["16000000.000000, the payment the engine prints for FIN on that same row.", "28000000.000000, EKO''s whole pre-deal 70 percent share of the 40000000.000000 well.", "12000000.000000, the rest of EKO''s own share after FIN''s payment.", "4000000.000000, the carry on that row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'EKO held 70 percent and pays what is left of its share after FIN''s payment: the engine returns 12000000.000000. 16000000.000000 is FIN''s payment, 28000000.000000 would be EKO paying its whole pre-deal share as though there were no farm-out, and 4000000.000000 is the carry, the part of EKO''s post-deal share that FIN pays.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 6, 'EKO holds 70 and PA 30 percent. A box states one event in which FIN pays 25 percent of the well to earn 30 percent. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal on events[0].farmineePaysPct: the share must be at or above 30, the interest held after the event.", "A result with a promote of minus five points, the farmor carrying the farminee for part of its own share.", "A refusal on events[0].earnedPct, saying the interest earned must be at most the 25 percent share paid.", "A result in which the share paid is raised to 30 with a reason line saying the promote is at its floor."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses a negative promote, in its own words: "events[0].farmineePaysPct must be at or above 30, the interest the farminee holds after the event (a promote of 0 or more); got 25". It names the share paid, the term that breaks the rule, and it never adjusts a stated share. A farminee paid for by the farmor would be a different trade, which the earning call does not compute.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 7, 'With EKO at 70 and PA at 30 percent, a box has FIN paying 75 percent of the well to earn 30. Why does the engine refuse it?', '["No share paid may exceed twice the interest earned, putting this deal''s ceiling at 60.", "A promote above 40 points is refused as a matter of law under the 2024 Regulations.", "The share paid may not pass 50 percent, the change-of-control line of PIA s.95(14), which 75 would cross.", "The share paid is capped at 70, the farmor''s pre-deal share: a farminee pays no other party''s share."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words: "events[0].farmineePaysPct must be at most 70, the farmor''s interest before the deal (the farminee pays no other party''s share); got 75". The limit is the farmor''s pre-deal share, since the farminee pays only the farmor''s side of the well. The engine states no ratio ceiling and no legal cap on a promote; the Regulations set none. The 50% of s.95(14) is a line on voting power in a holder and plays no part in the earning call.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 8, 'Suppose FIN is to earn 71 percent of the licence from EKO, whose participating interest before the deal is 70. Which field does the refusal name, and against what limit?', '["events[0].farmineePaysPct, at most 70, since earning more than the farmor holds needs a share paid above it.", "events[0].earnedPct, at most the farmor''s interest 70 less the 0 already earned.", "parties, since FIN and PA would pass 100 after the deal.", "farmor, since a farminee earning 71 percent would leave EKO with no participating interest to farm out."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s words: "events[0].earnedPct must be at most 70, the farmor''s interest 70 less 0 already earned; got 71". The interest earned comes out of the farmor''s alone, so the limit is the farmor''s interest less whatever earlier events earned, here none. The parties check reads the stated shares before the deal, and the farmor field names a party.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 9, 'HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual, page OT30048 (updated 1 May 2019), explains what it means by an obligation to undertake. What does it say the phrase covers?', '["Every case where the farmer in commits to bear his and the farmer out''s share of exploration or appraisal costs.", "Every case where the farmer out commits to drill a well at its own cost before any assignment is made.", "Only those cases where the farmer in pays cash to the farmer out for the sunk costs of the licence.", "Only work carried out after the grant of consent, which the manual counts as the start of the farm in."]'::jsonb, 0, 'OT30048 says the phrase is intended to cover all cases where the farmer in commits himself to bear his own and the farmer out''s share of the costs of exploration or appraisal work. The farmer out drilling alone is no farm-in, a cash reimbursement of sunk costs is described on OT30021 and OT30081, and the manual ties the phrase to no consent date.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 10, 'HMRC''s page OT30021 (updated 2 February 2021) separates two orders of events. Which order does it call a Farm in?', '["The work obligation completed first, and the assignment after it.", "An assignment made on the day the farminee pays its cash bonus, whatever the state of the work.", "An assignment at first production, once carried costs are recovered.", "The assignment made, subject to government consent, before the work is undertaken."]'::jsonb, 3, 'OT30021 says the assignment is normally made, subject to government consent, before the work is undertaken, and is called a Farm in; an agreement under which the work obligation is to be completed before the assignment is generally referred to as an earn-in. The manual ties neither order to a bonus date or to first production, which belongs to a development carry''s payback.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 11, 'Nothing has been drilled yet on a well of 40000000.000000: vesting is "per-event", 0 events are completed, and the stated terms (no cap; EKO 70, PA 30) oblige FIN to pay 40 percent for 30. Which figures do the event row and the totals print for FIN?', '["Both show 0.000000 until the event is completed.", "Both show 16000000.000000, since an obligation stated in the box counts as paid on signing.", "The row shows an obligation of 16000000.000000; the totals show 0.000000 paid.", "The row shows 0.000000 and the totals 16000000.000000, the whole obligation carried forward."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine reports every event''s split as the obligation and counts completed events only in its totals: the row prints 16000000.000000 for FIN, and the totals print 0.000000 paid and 0.000000 percent vested. Its reason line ends "(not completed: the obligation only)", and its vesting reason reports 0 of 1 event completed with 0% vesting. Nothing counts as paid on signing.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 12, 'On the Ekene Deep-1 row the share paid reads 40.000000 while the share of the gross cost FIN pays reads 39.565217. What does the engine''s reason line give as the cause?', '["FIN''s cash bonus of 2000000.000000 is netted off its well payment and shown as a lower share.", "The gross cost exceeds the cap 44000000 by 2000000, and the post-deal interests pay the excess.", "The past-cost reimbursement of 3600000.000000 is credited against FIN''s part of the well.", "The engine rounds every share paid down to the promote''s whole points before it splits the well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The reason line reads: "the gross cost exceeds the cap 44000000 by 2000000: the promote applies to 44000000; the excess is paid by the post-deal interests (FIN 30%, EKO 40%)". The course reads that line as a record here and leaves the working of caps to a later tier. The bonus and the reimbursement leave the split of the well alone, and the engine rounds no stated share.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 13, 'EKO holds the whole licence (100 percent). Its well costs 12000000.000000 with no cap, and FIN pays one third of it (the field prints 33.333333 percent) for 25 percent; the event is completed. What is EKO''s payment?', '["8000000.000000, EKO''s 75 percent less the carry FIN pays.", "9000000.000000, the 75 percent EKO holds after the event, applied to the whole well.", "4000000.000000, the payment the engine prints for FIN, the farminee, on that row.", "12000000.000000, the whole gross cost, since EKO is the only licence party."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns FIN paying 4000000.000000 and EKO 8000000.000000 on the 12000000.000000 well, with a carry of 1000000.000000. 9000000.000000 would be EKO paying its whole post-deal share with no carry, which ignores the part FIN pays. 4000000.000000 is FIN''s payment, and EKO pays the whole well only when no one farms in.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 14, 'A deal''s only well has no cap, and the learner leaves the cap out of the event in the box. What does the engine do?', '["It runs the event as uncapped: a missing cap means none.", "It runs the event with the gross cost as the cap, reached exactly.", "It refuses only when the share paid is above the interest earned.", "It refuses on events[0].cap, which must be stated, with on \"none\" when there is no cap."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words: events[0].cap must be an object { on } with on "none", "gross-cost" or "carry-amount" (no default); got nothing. Every event''s cap is a deal term with no default, so a missing cap is refused whatever the promote. A cap of "none" is a stated term; a missing cap is a gap the engine does not fill.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-earning-obligation', 15, 'On the completed Ekene Deep-1 well (EKO 70, PA 30 percent), which party''s payment is left unchanged when the learner changes the share FIN pays and runs the call again?', '["EKO''s, since the farmor''s payment is fixed by its pre-deal share of 70 percent.", "PA''s, since a party outside the trade pays its own share.", "FIN''s, since the farminee''s payment is fixed by the 30 percent interest it earns.", "All three payments, since the gross cost of 46000000.000000 is left as stated."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The other parties pay their own interests of each gross cost, so PA''s 13800000.000000 does not move with the share FIN pays. FIN''s payment follows the share it pays, and EKO pays what is left of its own share, so both move. The gross cost stays the same, and the split inside it changes between the farmor and the farminee.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 1, 'In the engine''s basis, what is the promote of an earning event?', '["The interest earned divided by the share of the gross cost paid, which gives a ratio below one.", "The share of the gross cost the farminee pays minus the interest it holds after the event, in points.", "The carry divided by the gross cost of the event, written as a percentage of the well.", "The share paid less the farmor''s participating interest before the deal, counted in points."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis reads: "promote = the share of the gross cost the farminee pays minus the interest it holds after the event (points); promote ratio = share paid / interest held". The ratio divides the share paid by the interest held, the other way up. The carry over the gross cost matches the points only when no cap bites, and the farmor''s pre-deal share is the ceiling on the share paid.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 2, 'FIN''s Ekene Deep-1 terms are 40 percent paid for 30 percent earned from EKO, and it holds 30 percent after the event. What promote does the engine return?', '["8.333333 points and a ratio of 1.333333, the promote of a third paid for a quarter earned.", "10.000000 points and a ratio of 1.724638, the promote-adjusted ratio printed in the tiles.", "0.000000 points and a ratio of 1.000000, since the cap on the gross cost removes the promote.", "10.000000 points and a ratio of 1.333333."]'::jsonb, 3, '40 paid less 30 held is 10.000000 points, and 40 over 30 is a ratio of 1.333333, as the engine''s row prints. 8.333333 points belongs to earn-third-for-a-quarter. 1.724638 is the promote-adjusted ratio, which counts the cash as well. The cap changes how the cost is split; the reason line still reports a promote of 10 points.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 3, 'A third for a quarter: FIN pays one third (33.333333 percent at six decimals) of a 12000000.000000 well (no cap) for 25 percent from EKO, the sole holder. What promote in points results?', '["8.333333 points, at the same 1.333333 ratio as forty for thirty.", "10.000000 points, the Ekene promote, since the ratio of 1.333333 is the same on both deals.", "25.000000 points, the whole participating interest earned counted as the farminee''s promote.", "33.333333 points, all of it promote."]'::jsonb, 0, '33.333333 paid less 25 held is 8.333333 points, and the ratio is 1.333333, the same as 40 for 30. Equal ratios on different interests give different points, which is why the course reads both measures. The interest earned is the farminee''s own share, and the promote subtracts it from the share paid.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 4, 'Heads up: on a 40000000.000000 well without a cap, FIN pays 30 percent and earns 30 from EKO (EKO 70, PA 30 beforehand). What is EKO''s bill for the well?', '["12000000.000000, FIN''s own share.", "28000000.000000, EKO''s pre-deal 70 percent of the well, since a heads-up deal moves no participating interest.", "16000000.000000, its own post-deal 40 percent of the well.", "4000000.000000, the carry FIN would pay on the same well at a share paid of 40 percent for 30."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Heads up, the farminee pays its own share and nothing of the farmor''s: the engine returns FIN 12000000.000000, EKO 16000000.000000, a promote of 0.000000 points and a carry of 0.000000. EKO holds 40 percent after the deal and pays all of it. A heads-up deal still moves 30 percent of the licence to FIN, and 4000000.000000 is the carry of a different deal.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 5, 'Case earn-full-carry. Terms: a licence split 70 and 30 between EKO and PA; FIN funds 70 percent of an uncapped 40000000.000000 well in return for 30 percent. How much carry results?', '["28000000.000000, the whole payment FIN makes for the well on these terms.", "12000000.000000, FIN''s own held 30 percent of the gross cost of the well.", "4000000.000000, ten points of the well, the promote of forty for thirty.", "16000000.000000, EKO''s whole post-deal share of the well."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns FIN paying 28000000.000000, EKO 0.000000 and a carry of 16000000.000000, with a promote of 40.000000 points and a ratio of 2.333333. The carry is FIN''s payment less its own held 30 percent of the well (12000000.000000), which is EKO''s whole 40 percent post-deal share. 4000000.000000 is the carry at 40 paid for 30.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 6, 'Every point of EKO''s participating interest (70 percent; PA holds the other 30) passes to FIN, which pays 70 percent of a 40000000.000000 well that has no cap. What promote does the engine report?', '["40.000000 points and a ratio of 2.333333, as in a full carry.", "0.000000 points: the whole share paid for the whole interest earned is heads up.", "70.000000 points, since FIN pays EKO''s whole pre-deal share of the well for the participating interest.", "None: the call is refused for a whole interest farmed out."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On earn-all-of-farmor the engine returns a promote of 0.000000 points, a ratio of 1.000000 and a carry of 0.000000, with FIN paying 28000000.000000 and EKO 0.000000. The promote is measured against the interest held after the event, here 70, so paying 70 is heads up. 40 points belongs to the full carry, which earns only 30, and earning the farmor''s whole interest is accepted.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 7, 'Which quantity does the engine''s basis call the carry?', '["What the farminee pays minus its own held interest of the gross cost.", "The farmor''s pre-deal share of the gross cost less what the farmor pays after the deal is struck.", "The promote''s points times the farmor''s pre-deal share.", "Cash: the bonus plus the reimbursement."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis reads: "carry = what the farminee pays minus its own held interest of the gross cost: the part of the farmor''s post-deal share the farminee pays". The farmor''s pre-deal share less its payment also counts the participating interest it gave up, the points are a share of the gross cost, and the bonus and the reimbursement are cash beside the carry in the consideration.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 8, 'A farminee''s payment is 16000000.000000 and its interest after the event is 30 percent; the well cost 40000000.000000 with no cap (EKO 70, PA 30 before the deal). By the basis rule, what carry does the engine report?', '["16000000.000000, the whole of FIN''s payment for the well.", "12000000.000000, which is what EKO pays for the same well after the deal.", "4000000.000000: the payment less FIN''s own 12000000.000000.", "28000000.000000, EKO''s pre-deal share of the well, which the farm-out carries."]'::jsonb, 2, 'FIN''s own share of the well is 30 percent of 40000000.000000, 12000000.000000; the carry is what it pays above that, 4000000.000000, as the engine returns on earn-bonus-and-reimbursement. With no cap this is 10.000000 points of the gross cost. 12000000.000000 is also EKO''s payment, and a farminee pays only part of the farmor''s share.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 9, 'The engine''s Ekene carry is 4400000.000000 although the promote is 10 points of a 46000000.000000 well. Given the deal''s gross-cost cap of 44000000.000000 (overrun rule "post-deal-interests"), what cost does the reason line apply the promote to?', '["46000000.000000, the whole gross cost of the success well in the fixture.", "44000000.000000, the capped cost.", "40000000.000000, the dry-hole cost of the exploration well in the fixture.", "2000000.000000, the part of the gross cost above the cap on this well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The reason line reads that the gross cost exceeds the cap 44000000 by 2000000 and "the promote applies to 44000000", so the carry is 10 points of 44000000.000000, which the engine returns as 4400000.000000. The promote in points is a term of the deal; the carry is money that also depends on the cap, which a later tier works in full.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 10, 'Two worked cases pay the same promote ratio of 1.333333: forty paid for thirty on a 40000000.000000 well, and a third paid for a quarter on a 12000000.000000 well. How do their promotes in points compare?', '["Both are 10.000000 points, since equal ratios give equal points.", "Both are 8.333333 points, the ratio fixing the points.", "10.000000 against 25.000000 points.", "They differ: 10.000000 points against 8.333333, since the participating interests differ."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns 10.000000 points on earn-cap-gross-below and 8.333333 on earn-third-for-a-quarter, each with a ratio of 1.333333. The ratio compares deals of different sizes; the points turn straight into money on a stated gross cost. Neither measure fixes the other, since each depends on the interest held after the event.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 11, 'The earn-third-for-a-quarter reason line prints FIN''s share paid with every digit of the double that was stated, while the field prints 33.333333. Which does the course tell a learner to reason with?', '["The field at six decimals; a message prints a stated input exactly as it was given.", "The message, the engine''s own words.", "Neither of them: round the share to the nearest whole percent first.", "The message''s figure rounded to the cent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course''s precision rule: inside a message the engine prints a stated input as it was given, and every numeric field keeps full precision, quoted at six decimals. The course reasons with the field, 33.333333, and quotes a message only verbatim. Rounding to a whole point changes the deal, and cent rounding applies to money in a reason.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 12, 'The refusal for a share paid of 25 against an interest earned of 30 names 30 as the lower limit. What is that 30?', '["PA''s participating interest, 30 percent.", "EKO''s interest after the deal less ten points.", "What FIN holds once the event vests, the 30 percent it earns.", "A minimum promote ratio of 30 percent set by the Assignment of Interests Regulations, 2024."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The message reads that the share "must be at or above 30, the interest the farminee holds after the event (a promote of 0 or more)". The limit is the figure the promote is measured against, the farminee''s interest after the event. PA''s 30 percent is the same number by coincidence, EKO holds 40 after the deal, and the Regulations set no promote.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 13, 'Along the edge of the promote, how far can the share paid rise on a licence where EKO, the farmor, holds 70 and PA 30 percent, and what does EKO pay at that edge?', '["70 percent, with EKO paying 0.000000.", "100 percent, with EKO and PA both carried by the farminee for their shares.", "40 percent, the promote ceiling of a single event under the 2024 Regulations.", "75 percent, with PA carried for five points of its own share of the well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The farminee pays only the farmor''s side of the well. At a share of 70 the engine returns EKO paying 0.000000 and a carry of 16000000.000000 (earn-full-carry); a share one point above is refused, with 70 named as the farmor''s interest before the deal. PA pays its own share throughout, and no text sets a ceiling of 40 on a promote.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 14, 'In dollars, what carry does FIN pay for sole holder EKO when a third of an uncapped 12000000.000000 well buys FIN a quarter of the licence?', '["4000000.000000, which is the whole of what FIN pays for the 12000000.000000 well.", "3000000.000000, FIN''s own 25 percent.", "8000000.000000, what EKO pays for the well after the farminee''s payment is made.", "1000000.000000, which is the promote in points applied to the well."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns a carry of 1000000.000000: FIN pays 4000000.000000, its own 25 percent is 3000000.000000, and the difference is the carry. With no cap that is the promote in points applied to the gross cost: a third less a quarter of 12000000.000000, the promote whose field prints 8.333333. 8000000.000000 is EKO''s payment.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-promote-and-its-ratio', 15, 'Deals are often quoted as a ratio, such as forty for thirty. What does the engine''s promote ratio divide by what?', '["The interest earned by the share of the gross cost that the farminee pays for it.", "The share paid by the participating interest held after the event.", "The carry by the farminee''s payment.", "The share paid by the farmor''s pre-deal share."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis reads "promote ratio = share paid / interest held", so forty for thirty is 1.333333 and heads up is 1.000000. The inverse would read below one for every promoted deal. The carry over the payment is no term the engine reports, and the farmor''s pre-deal share is the limit on the share paid.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 1, 'EKO''s past costs on the Ekene licence are 12000000.000000, and the deal states that FIN reimburses 30 percent of them. What reimbursement does the engine return?', '["12000000.000000, the whole of EKO''s past costs, since a farminee reimburses sunk costs in full.", "4400000.000000, the carry, which the engine counts as the reimbursement of EKO''s share of the well.", "3600000.000000, the stated share of the stated amount.", "2700000.000000, the reimbursement of the earn-bonus-and-reimbursement case at the same 30 percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reimbursement is the stated share of the stated past costs: 30 percent of 12000000.000000, which the engine returns as 3600000.000000 and writes in its reason as "past-cost reimbursement 3600000 (30% of 12000000)". The share reimbursed is its own stated term, so a full reimbursement would be stated as 100. The carry is a separate part of the consideration, and 2700000.000000 is 30 percent of 9000000.000000.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 2, 'On the Ekene Deep deal the carry is 4400000.000000, the cash bonus 2000000.000000 and the reimbursement 30 percent of past costs of 12000000.000000, with the one event completed. What consideration to EKO does the engine return?', '["10000000.000000: carry, cash bonus and reimbursement added.", "23800000.000000, FIN''s outlay for the deal, which also counts its payment for the well.", "5600000.000000, the bonus and the reimbursement.", "4400000.000000, the carry on its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason reads "consideration to EKO: carry 4400000 + cash bonus 2000000 + past-cost reimbursement 3600000 (30% of 12000000) = 10000000". The consideration is what the farmor receives, so FIN''s own share of the well stays out of it; 23800000.000000 is FIN''s outlay. The cash alone and the carry alone each leave out a part.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 3, 'The case earn-bonus-and-reimbursement states a bonus of 1500000.000000 and 30 percent of 9000000.000000 in past costs reimbursed; its well (40000000.000000, no cap; EKO 70, PA 30) is paid 40 percent by FIN for 30, and is completed. What is EKO''s consideration?', '["20200000.000000, FIN''s outlay: its well payment together with the cash.", "4000000.000000, the carry and nothing else.", "9000000.000000, the stated past costs, which the farmor recovers in full from FIN.", "8200000.000000, the carry, the bonus and the reimbursement together."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns a carry of 4000000.000000, a reimbursement of 2700000.000000 (30 percent of 9000000.000000) and a consideration of 8200000.000000, written as "carry 4000000 + cash bonus 1500000 + past-cost reimbursement 2700000 (30% of 9000000) = 8200000". 20200000.000000 is FIN''s outlay, which also counts its own share of the well. Only the stated 30 percent of the past costs is reimbursed.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 4, 'FIN pays 18200000.000000 for the Ekene Deep-1 well, a cash bonus of 2000000.000000 and a reimbursement of 3600000.000000, the event completed. What does the engine return as the farminee outlay?', '["18200000.000000, the well payment alone, which is FIN''s whole share of the deal.", "23800000.000000: the well payment of 18200000.000000 plus the bonus and the reimbursement.", "10000000.000000, the consideration EKO receives from the farm-out.", "46000000.000000, the gross cost of the well that FIN is paying toward."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The outlay is the farminee''s well payment plus the cash bonus and the reimbursement: 18200000.000000 + 2000000.000000 + 3600000.000000, which the engine returns as 23800000.000000. The well payment alone leaves out the cash, the consideration counts the carry in place of FIN''s whole payment, and the gross cost is shared among three parties.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 5, 'FIN''s outlay on the Ekene Deep deal is 23800000.000000 for a completed well of 46000000.000000, and it vests 30 percent. What equivalent working interest does the engine return?', '["51.739130, the heads-up share FIN''s outlay would buy.", "39.565217, the share of the gross cost FIN pays for the well, which leaves the cash out of the figure.", "30.000000, the participating interest FIN vests, which the engine reports as what the outlay buys.", "40.000000, the stated share paid, which the engine reports as the equivalent interest of the deal."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis reads "equivalent working interest = (farminee pays + cash bonus + past-cost reimbursement) / gross cost of the completed events x 100: the heads-up interest that would cost the farminee the same", and the engine returns 51.739130. 39.565217 is the well payment alone over the gross cost. 30.000000 is what FIN actually vests, and 40.000000 is the stated share paid.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 6, 'What promote-adjusted ratio goes with an equivalent working interest of 51.739130 when 30 percent vests?', '["1.333333, the promote ratio of forty for thirty, which reads the well terms alone and leaves out the cash.", "1.000000, since cash leaves the ratio alone.", "1.724638, the equivalent working interest over the 30 percent vested.", "2.333333, a full carry''s ratio of seventy for thirty."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis defines the promote-adjusted ratio as the equivalent working interest over the vested interest, and the engine returns 1.724638. The plain promote ratio of 1.333333 looks at the well terms alone; the adjusted ratio adds the cash, so it rises above it. 2.333333 is the ratio of paying 70 for 30.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 7, 'Add a bonus of 1500000.000000 and a reimbursement of 2700000.000000 to FIN''s 16000000.000000 payment for an uncapped, drilled 40000000.000000 well that vests 30 percent. Which two figures does the engine print for the heads-up equivalent and its ratio?', '["40.000000 and 1.333333, the share paid and the promote ratio before any cash is counted.", "51.739130 and 1.724638, the equivalent figures that belong to the Ekene Deep deal.", "50.500000, with an adjusted ratio of 1.333333.", "50.500000, with a promote-adjusted ratio of 1.683333."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The outlay is 20200000.000000, which over the 40000000.000000 well is 50.500000 percent, and over the 30 percent vested a ratio of 1.683333 (engine). 40.000000 and 1.333333 are the figures with no cash. The Ekene figures come from a different well and different cash. The promote-adjusted ratio moves with the cash, so it cannot stay at 1.333333, the plain promote ratio the event row prints.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 8, 'A learner sets the cash bonus control to "not stated" and runs the call, then sets it to 0 and runs again. What does the engine return each time?', '["A result both times, since the engine reads a missing bonus as a bonus of 0 and says so in a reason.", "First a refusal on cashBonus, a term with no default; then a result with a reason line reporting the bonus as stated at 0.", "A refusal both times, since a bonus stated as 0 is treated as no bonus stated at all.", "First a result with no bonus line, then a refusal on cashBonus for a bonus of 0."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A missing bonus is a gap in the terms, and the engine refuses it in its own words: "cashBonus must be a finite number at or above 0; got nothing". A bonus of 0 is a stated term, which the engine accepts and reports in a reason line of its own: "cash bonus: none (stated as 0)". The two are different things, which is why the panel''s control offers both.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 9, 'A farm-out carries no reimbursement of past costs. How must the box state its past costs?', '["By leaving pastCosts out of the box, which the engine reads as a deal with no reimbursement.", "As amount 0 alone, with no share stated.", "As an object with amount and reimbursedPct both 0.", "As reimbursedPct 0, the amount left out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s words: "pastCosts must be an object { amount, reimbursedPct } (both 0 when the deal has no reimbursement; no default); got nothing". Both terms are required, and a deal with no reimbursement states both as 0. Leaving the object out is refused, and an object with one of the two terms is incomplete.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 10, 'HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual, page OT30081 (updated 1 May 2019), says how a reimbursement of earlier costs should be treated. How?', '["As cash consideration for the licence interest acquired.", "As a loan the farmor repays out of production.", "As part of the farminee''s work obligation, the phrase the manual explains on OT30048.", "As a fee for assigning rights, which the Act makes non-deductible for tax."]'::jsonb, 0, 'OT30081 says the reimbursement should be treated as cash consideration for the licence interest acquired, and OT30021 describes the payment itself as a cash reimbursement for sunk costs relating to the proportionate interest acquired. A loan repaid from production is closer to a development carry, the work obligation is the undertaking to bear costs, and the non-deductible fees are the consent fees.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 11, 'Take the no-cap well of 40000000.000000 with FIN paying 40 percent for 30 and a cash bonus of 1500000.000000. The learner changes the bonus to 0 and runs the call again. What happens to FIN''s payment for the well?', '["It falls by 1500000.000000, since the bonus is netted off the payment for the well.", "It rises by 1500000.000000, since the bonus is paid through the farminee''s share of the well.", "It falls to 12000000.000000, since the promote goes with the bonus and FIN pays heads up.", "It stays 16000000.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The bonus moves money from the farminee to the farmor on top of the well terms and does not change the split: FIN pays 16000000.000000 of the well whether the bonus is 1500000.000000 or 0. The bonus changes the consideration and the outlay only. The promote is set by the share paid and the interest held, which the bonus leaves alone.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 12, 'How does the course keep the consideration apart from the value of the transaction?', '["They are one figure: the 2024 Regulations charge the fee on the consideration the engine adds up, carry and all.", "The consideration adds up deal terms; the value of the transaction is a separately stated amount that reg. 19(3) charges the fee on.", "The value of the transaction is the carry alone, since the carry is the part of the price paid in work.", "The consideration is the value of the transaction plus the consent fee charged on it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocabulary rule makes the consideration what the farmor receives (the carry, the cash bonus and the reimbursement) and the value of the transaction the amount reg. 19(3) charges the fee on, which a deal states with its source. The engine does not decide which consideration of a farm-out counts, so neither figure is read off the other in this course.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 13, 'Before the well is drilled (0 events completed, vesting "per-event"), what consideration does the engine total for EKO on a deal of 40 paid for 30 on an uncapped 40000000.000000 well with no bonus and no past costs?', '["4000000.000000, the carry of the obligation shown on the event row for the well.", "16000000.000000, FIN''s obligation for the uncompleted well, counted as consideration.", "4000000.000000, owed from signing day.", "0.000000, since the totals count completed events only."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The consideration is a total, and the totals count completed events only: the engine''s line reads "consideration to EKO: carry 0 + cash bonus 0 + past-cost reimbursement 0 (0% of 0) = 0". The event row still shows the carry of 4000000.000000 the obligation would bring. FIN''s payment is its outlay, and nothing counts on signing.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 14, 'In the engine''s equivalent working interest, what is the farminee''s outlay divided by?', '["The gross cost of the completed events, times 100 to give a percentage.", "The farmor''s participating interest before the deal, 70 on Ekene.", "The consideration to the farmor, carry and cash included.", "The participating interest vested, as in the ratio."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis divides the farminee''s payment, cash bonus and reimbursement by the gross cost of the completed events and multiplies by 100: the heads-up interest that would cost the farminee the same. Dividing that figure by the vested interest gives the promote-adjusted ratio.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-cash-bonus-and-reimbursement', 15, 'Heads up and cash-free: 30 percent of a drilled 40000000.000000 well, no cap, buys FIN 30 percent, with bonus and past costs both stated as 0. What equivalent working interest results?', '["40.000000, the farmor''s post-deal share, which the farminee''s payment matches on a heads-up deal.", "0.000000, since a deal with no cash has no equivalent working interest for the engine to report.", "30.000000, the share paid.", "51.739130, the Ekene figure, since the equivalent interest is a property of the licence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With no cash the outlay is the well payment, 12000000.000000, which over the 40000000.000000 well is 30.000000 percent, and the promote-adjusted ratio is 1.000000 (engine, earn-heads-up). A deal with no cash still has an outlay, EKO''s post-deal share is its own, and 51.739130 belongs to the Ekene deal''s terms.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 1, 'The Ekene licence is a petroleum prospecting licence (PPL). Whose consent does its farm-out need, and how is that consent given?', '["The Commission''s own prior written consent, since the licence is still at the exploration stage.", "The Minister''s prior written consent, granted on the Commission''s recommendation.", "The Minister''s alone, with no role for the Commission.", "No consent at all, since a farm-out assigns nothing until the farminee''s work is finished."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Act gives the decision to the Minister and the recommendation to the Commission (PIA s.95(2)), and the application is made to the Commission (AOI Regulations 2024 reg. 4(2)). The Commission''s own consent is the rule for a petroleum exploration licence, a different licence type. HMRC''s manual notes that even a farm in, assigned before the work, is made subject to government consent.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 2, 'Which authority gives consent to the assignment of an interest in a petroleum exploration licence (PEL)?', '["The Minister, on the Commission''s recommendation, as for a PPL or a PML.", "The Minister alone, with consent deemed after 60 working days of silence.", "No authority: an exploration licence can be assigned without any consent.", "The Commission, by its prior written consent (PIA s.95(15); AOI Regulations 2024 reg. 16(c))."]'::jsonb, 3, 'PIA s.95(15) says a PEL holder shall not assign without the prior written consent of the Commission, and reg. 16(c) applies the same to a change in control of a PEL holder. The Minister''s consent on the Commission''s recommendation is the PPL and PML route. The 60 working days of s.95(7)(b) run on the Minister''s silence after a recommendation.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 3, 'Under PIA s.95(14), when does acquiring voting power in a licence holder become a change of control?', '["When it reaches 50% exactly, the point at which the buyers hold half of the voting power.", "When one buyer on its own passes 50%, since the Act counts no persons acting together.", "When the stake exceeds 50%, one buyer or several in concert.", "When it passes 50% of the participating interest in the licence, whatever the voting power."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Act defines a change of control as any person or persons acting jointly or in concert acquiring a percentage of the voting power of the holder that "exceeds 50%" at any time. Exactly half does not exceed it. Persons acting together count, and the test is voting power in the holder, a company, with no reference to a participating interest in the licence. The engine records the figure as changeOfControlAbovePct, 50.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 4, 'A buyer takes control of the company that holds EKO''s participating interest, and no farm-out is signed. How does the Act treat the purchase?', '["As an assignment: s.95(3) deems a change of control in a holder to be one.", "As a private share sale outside the Act, since the participating interest stays with the same holder.", "A farm-out under s.94(8)(b), since control of the holder moves.", "A PEL matter for the Commission alone to decide."]'::jsonb, 0, 'PIA s.95(3) says a change of control in the holder of a licence or lease shall be deemed to be an assignment, so the purchase needs consent as a sale of the participating interest would. The licence staying with the same holder is exactly the case s.95(3) reaches. A farm-out is an agreement with a third party to work the licence, and the Ekene licence is a PPL.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 5, 'Regulation 3(3) of the Nigerian Upstream Petroleum (Assignment of Interests) Regulations, 2024 requires the Minister''s prior written consent to a change in control of a holder. Which holder does it except?', '["A holder whose change in control stays at or below the 50% line of PIA s.95(14).", "A licensee, lessee or indirect controller listed on a public exchange.", "A holder incorporated in Nigeria, the condition the Minister may set under s.95(11)(a).", "Any holder of a petroleum mining lease."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Regulation 3(3) applies to a change in the control of a company that holds an interest in a licence or lease, other than a licensee, lessee or indirect controller of a licence or lease listed on a public exchange. A change at or below the line is no change of control to begin with, incorporation in Nigeria is a condition the Minister may set on a transferee, and a mining lease is one of the licences the rule covers.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 6, 'What must a holder''s notification of intention to the Commission state under reg. 4(4)(b) of the 2024 Regulations?', '["The value of the transaction, the consent fee and the date by which the fee will be paid.", "The field development plan the farmee will present for the licence or lease area.", "The reason for the assignment, the method of the transaction, and its possible technical and economic benefits.", "The participating interests before and after the deal, and the promote of each earning event."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Regulation 4(4)(b) asks for a statement of "the reason for the intended assignment, the method intended to be used for the conduct of the transaction, and possible technical and economic benefits derivable from the transaction". The earning calculator gives figures a notification could carry, but the regulation names no promote, fee or date. A field development plan is the marginal field condition of PIA s.94(5).'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 7, 'What happens when the Commission does not answer a notification of intention within 15 working days?', '["The notification lapses, and the holder must notify the Commission again before applying.", "The Minister decides it directly, with no recommendation from the Commission.", "The deal proceeds as an earn-in, with the assignment put off until the work is done.", "The application is deemed approved (reg. 4(7))."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Regulation 4(7) says the Commission shall approve or disapprove the transaction within 15 working days from receipt of the notification of intention, failing which the application is deemed approved. Nothing lapses and the Minister gains no direct role. An earn-in is a deal''s own order of events, which HMRC''s manual describes; no regulation turns silence into one.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 8, 'What does PIA s.95(7)(b) provide when the Minister does not respond to an application?', '["Consent is deemed granted 60 working days after receipt of the Commission''s recommendation.", "The application is deemed refused after 60 working days, and the holder may apply again after a year.", "The Commission grants the consent in the Minister''s place.", "Consent waits until the fee is paid in full."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 95(7)(b): where no response on the application has been received within 60 working days from the receipt of the recommendation of the Commission, the consent of the Minister shall be deemed to have been granted. Silence counts as consent, and the Commission recommends while the Minister decides. Payment in full is a separate condition, in reg. 19(6).'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 9, 'How long does reg. 18(3) of the 2024 Regulations give the Commission to answer an application to assign an interest in a PEL?', '["15 working days, the same period the Commission has for a notification of intention.", "60 calendar days, after which the application is deemed approved.", "No period: the Regulations leave a PEL''s timing open.", "60 working days from receipt, with its reason given in writing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Regulation 18(3): the Commission shall communicate the reason for the refusal or approval of an assignment of an interest in a PEL in writing to the applicant within 60 working days of the receipt of the application. The 15 working days are for a notification of intention under reg. 4(7), and the period is counted in working days; reg. 18(3) as the course quotes it names no deemed approval.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 10, 'Which provisions the course quotes say that fees for an assignment, or for assigning rights, are not deductible for tax?', '["Only reg. 19(5) of the 2024 Regulations, since the Act itself is silent on the tax treatment of any fee.", "PIA s.95(12), AOI Regulations 2024 reg. 19(5), and PIA s.264(f) and s.302(12)(c).", "None of them: the fee is deductible as a cost of the deal.", "Only s.95(12) of the Act, which covers every tax at once."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 95(12) says the fee shall not be tax deductible, reg. 19(5) says the same of the processing fee, consent fee and premium, and the tax sections list fees paid for assigning rights to another party as not deductible: s.264(f) for hydrocarbon tax and s.302(12)(c) for companies income tax. The Nigerian fiscal system as a whole is the Petroleum Industry Act course''s subject.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 11, 'Under reg. 19(6) of the 2024 Regulations, what is the consent of the Minister and the Commission held back until?', '["Full payment of the application and processing fees.", "Completion of the farminee''s first earning event, as in an earn-in.", "The Minister''s signature on the field development plan of the licence.", "The end of the Commission''s 15 working days for a notification of intention."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Regulation 19(6): the consent of the Minister and the Commission shall not be granted until the appropriate application and processing fees have been fully paid. The Professional tier computes the fee. The earning events, a field development plan and the notification period are separate matters with their own texts.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 12, 'What does PIA s.233(10) require of a farm out agreement, and what does the engine do with it?', '["A decommissioning fund held by the farmor alone, which the engine sizes from the gross cost of the well.", "A plan for the farmee''s field development, which the engine checks against the earning events.", "A decommissioning and abandonment plan funded in whole or in part by the incoming parties; the engine computes no such share.", "Nothing on decommissioning: s.233(10) deals only with the consent fee on the value of the transaction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 233(10): where the licensee or lessee is a party to a farm out agreement with one or more third parties, a decommissioning and abandonment plan funded in whole or in part by the applicable third parties shall be provided for in the agreement. The engine computes no decommissioning share and treats it as a concept only. A field development plan is s.94(5), and the fee is s.95(12).'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 13, 'What does PIA s.94(5) add to the Commission''s consent to the farm-out of a marginal field?', '["The farmee must be a company incorporated in Nigeria and listed on a public exchange.", "The farmor must reimburse the farmee''s past costs before any consent is given.", "The Minister must consent to it as well.", "The farmee must present a field development plan."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 94(5) makes the consent of the Commission to a marginal field farm-out subject, amongst others, to the farmee presenting a field development plan. Incorporation in Nigeria is a condition the Minister may set on a transferee (s.95(11)(a)), a listed holder is the exception in reg. 3(3), and a reimbursement runs from the farminee to the farmor.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 14, 'What does the farmout engine compute about consent?', '["Nothing: it reports who consents in a basis line, and refuses a PEL under the gazetted fee basis.", "It decides whether the Minister''s consent is deemed granted, from the dates of the recommendation and the silence.", "Deemed approvals: it grants one once 15 working days pass in silence.", "It checks each change of control against the 50% line of s.95(14)."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine computes no consent. Its fee basis carries the rule in its own words: "a PPL or PML assignment needs the prior written consent of the Minister on the Commission''s recommendation (PIA s.95(1) and (2)); a change of control above 50% is an assignment (s.95(3) and (14)); a PEL assignment needs the consent of the Commission (s.95(15); reg. 16)". No term in a box states who owns a holder''s voting securities.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-consent-process-in-words', 15, 'Which condition on a transferee does PIA s.95(11)(a) name among those the Minister may set?', '["That it holds no other participating interest in a licence or lease in Nigeria.", "That it presents a field development plan before the assignment takes effect.", "That it is a company incorporated in Nigeria.", "That it is listed on a public exchange, so that a change in its control needs no consent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Section 95(11)(a) reads "(a) is a company incorporated in Nigeria ;" as the Act prints it, one of the conditions the Minister may set. A field development plan is the marginal field condition of s.94(5), and a public listing is the exception in reg. 3(3) to the consent needed for a change in control. No text the course reads bars a transferee from holding other licences.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 1, 'Ekene Deep, completed: FIN earns 30 percent from EKO under "per-event" vesting (EKO 70 and PA 30 before the deal). Which shares does the engine report afterwards?', '["EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 30.000000.", "EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 0.000000, since the payments leave the licence shares unchanged.", "EKO 40.000000, PA 20.000000, FIN 40.000000, with FIN''s 40 percent share paid vesting in full.", "EKO 70.000000, PA 0.000000, FIN 30.000000, with PA''s share passing to the farminee."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The farminee''s participating interest comes out of the farmor''s alone: EKO falls from 70.000000 to 40.000000, FIN rises from 0.000000 to 30.000000 and PA keeps its 30.000000 (engine). The share paid sets what FIN pays for the well; the interest earned sets what vests. A completed event under "per-event" moves the stated interest.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 2, 'EKO holds the whole licence. FIN earns 25 percent by paying 33.333333 percent of a 12000000.000000 well with no cap; the event is completed under "per-event" vesting. What participating interests after the deal does the engine return?', '["EKO 100.000000 and FIN 25.000000, since a sole holder keeps its whole licence and FIN''s share is new.", "EKO 75.000000 and FIN 33.333333, the share of the well FIN paid.", "EKO 75.000000 and FIN 25.000000: the interest earned moves from the farmor.", "EKO 70.000000 and FIN 30.000000, as on the Ekene licence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On earn-third-for-a-quarter the engine returns EKO 75.000000 and FIN 25.000000. The rule is the same with no other party: the participating interest earned moves from the farmor to the farminee, and the shares still sum to 100. 33.333333 is the share of the well FIN pays, and 70 and 30 are the Ekene deal''s figures.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 3, 'Case earn-full-carry: FIN funds 70 percent of an uncapped 40000000.000000 well, earning 30 percent from EKO''s 70 (PA holds 30); the well is drilled. Which licence shares result?', '["EKO 0.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 70.000000, since FIN paid EKO''s whole pre-deal share of the well.", "EKO 40.000000, PA 20.000000, FIN 40.000000, the promote taking ten points of PA''s share.", "Unchanged: EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 0.000000.", "The Ekene table again: EKO 40.000000 with PA and FIN at 30.000000 each."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The interests after do not depend on the price: FIN earns 30 percent, so the engine returns EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 30.000000, the same table as the Ekene deal. The share paid of 70 sets FIN''s payment and the carry of 16000000.000000. PA is outside the trade, and a completed event moves the interest earned.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 4, 'Nothing is drilled yet (0 events completed, rule "per-event") on a deal that would move 30 percent from EKO to FIN, PA holding 30. What table of participating interests does the engine print?', '["EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 30.000000, vesting on signing.", "EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 0.000000: nothing vests until the event is completed.", "None, since the engine refuses an event that has not been completed yet.", "EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 30.000000, pending."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On earn-none-completed the engine returns EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 0.000000, and its reason reports 0 of 1 event completed with 0% vesting. Under "per-event" each completed event vests its stated interest, so nothing moves before completion. An uncompleted event is a result with a reason. Shares that sum to more than 100 cannot be a licence.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 5, 'Which basis line does the engine print for the vesting rule "per-event"?', '["nothing vests until every event is completed (an earn in: the work before the assignment, HMRC OT30021)", "each event vests its stated interest on signing, before any work is done (a farm in, HMRC OT30020)", "each completed event vests its stated interest (a farm in: assignment with the work, HMRC OT30021)", "each completed event vests the share paid, in points (HMRC OT30048)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s basis for "per-event" reads "each completed event vests its stated interest (a farm in: assignment with the work, HMRC OT30021)". The line beginning "nothing vests" is the basis of "all-events", the other rule. No rule vests on signing, and what vests is the interest earned: the share paid sets the payment.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 6, 'A box states the vesting rule as "on-signing". What does the engine return?', '["A refusal on vesting: it must be one of \"per-event\", \"all-events\".", "A result in which the interest vests at signing, the order HMRC calls a Farm in on page OT30021.", "A result read as \"per-event\", since one event is stated.", "A refusal on eventsCompleted, which must be 0 at signing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s words: vesting must be one of "per-event", "all-events"; got "on-signing". The vesting rule is a stated term with no default, and a rule the engine does not know is refused by name. HMRC''s farm in is assigned before the work, yet the engine''s "per-event" rule still vests an event on completion, as its basis says.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 7, 'A deal has a single earning event, and the box states eventsCompleted as 2. What does the engine return?', '["A result counting the event twice, so FIN vests 60 percent of the licence.", "A refusal: at most 1, the number of events.", "A result read as 1 completed event, with a reason noting the extra count.", "A refusal on vesting, since two completions need the rule \"all-events\"."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s words: "eventsCompleted must be at most 1, the number of events; got 2". The events completed is a stated count from 0 up to the number of events, and the engine neither doubles nor trims it. The vesting rule is a separate term, checked on its own.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 8, 'Reading the Ekene participating interests after the farm-in, which two checks does the course make?', '["They sum to more than 100, since FIN''s participating interest is added on top of the licence.", "PA''s share fell by the carry, and EKO''s by the promote.", "FIN''s share equals the 40 percent it paid, and EKO holds the rest.", "They still sum to 100, and FIN''s 30 percent came out of EKO''s share alone while PA kept its 30."]'::jsonb, 3, 'EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 30.000000 sum to 100, and only the farmor''s share moved (engine). A farminee joins inside the licence, so the shares stay whole. The carry and the promote set money, and PA is outside the trade. FIN holds the 30 percent it earned; the 40 percent it paid sets its payment.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 9, 'An event not yet completed comes back with a vested participating interest tile of 0.000000. Is that a refusal?', '["No: it is a result, with the obligation split and a reason saying nothing has vested.", "Yes: the engine refuses a deal whose events are not all completed, naming eventsCompleted as the field.", "Yes: a tile of 0.000000 marks a refused term.", "No: it is a warning, and the engine drops the uncompleted event from every table of the result."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course''s rule: a result returned with a reason (an event not completed) is a result, and no refusal. The engine returns the split of the obligation, with FIN''s 16000000.000000 on earn-none-completed, and a reason saying 0% vests. A refusal returns no figures, and the event stays in the row as the obligation.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 10, 'In the course''s checklist for reading a farm-out, which line does the earning calculator leave to the texts?', '["The consideration and the equivalent working interest, read from the Act before the tiles are checked.", "The participating interests after the deal, from the table.", "The consent needed and the fee basis, read from the Act and the 2024 Regulations.", "The vesting rule and the events completed, from the box."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The checklist''s line 8, the consent needed and the fee basis, is read from the texts, since the earning calculator computes no consent; the Professional tier computes the fee. The consideration, the equivalent working interest and the interests after are results the engine returns, and the vesting rule and the events completed are stated inputs.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 11, 'Which lines of the course''s checklist are inputs, read off the deal before any result is looked at?', '["The consideration, the equivalent working interest and the interests after the deal.", "Every line, since each figure the calculator prints is a stated input of the deal.", "Only the licence parties, since the engine computes every other line from them.", "The parties, the roles, the events, the vesting and the cash."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lines 1 to 5 (the licence and its parties, the farmor and the farminee, each event''s terms, the vesting rule and the events completed, the cash bonus and past costs) are inputs, read off the deal and checked against the box. The consideration, the equivalent working interest and the interests after are results. The engine computes nothing a deal does not state.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 12, 'Before copying a payment from the earning calculator, which check does the course advise on an event''s payments?', '["Check that the farminee''s payment equals the share paid times the dry-hole cost of the well.", "Add the farminee''s, the farmor''s and the other parties'' payments, and check they make up the gross cost.", "Check that the farmor''s payment equals the consideration it receives from the deal.", "Confirm that each payment ends in whole millions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the Ekene well, 18200000.000000 + 14000000.000000 + 13800000.000000 = 46000000.000000 (derived), the gross cost; if the payments do not make up the well, a term has been misread. The dry-hole cost is no term of the earning event, the farmor''s payment is what it pays for the well, and the consideration is what it receives.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 13, 'Why do the full carry case and the Ekene deal end with the same participating interests after the deal?', '["Both earn 30 percent from EKO, and the interest earned alone decides the table.", "Both pay the same promote of 10 points, which fixes each party''s share after the deal.", "Both carry a cash bonus of 2000000.000000 paid on signing.", "Both have a cap on the gross cost of the well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The promote, the carry and the cash decide what the participating interest costs; the interest earned decides the table (engine: EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000, FIN 30.000000 on both). The full carry''s promote is 40 points against Ekene''s 10, it states no bonus, and it has no cap.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 14, 'The refusal for FIN earning 71 from EKO''s 70 names "the farmor''s interest 70 less 0 already earned". What does the 0 already earned count?', '["The cash bonus already paid to the farmor, stated as 0 in the box.", "Promote points already taken on the well.", "The share of the gross cost already paid by PA, the other party.", "What earlier events of the same deal earned, none here."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The interest earned comes out of the farmor''s alone, so the limit is the farmor''s interest less what earlier events already earned. With one event that is nothing; the count belongs to deals with more than one earning event, which the Professional tier works. Cash, the promote and PA''s payments play no part in the limit.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-interests-after-the-deal', 15, 'The Associate capstone grades figures that the earning calculator returns. Where does its deal come from?', '["The Ekene Deep deal, with the figures the lessons print, so that a learner can copy them across.", "A real Nigerian farm-out from the regulator''s register, with the parties'' names replaced.", "Its own synthetic deal, printed nowhere in the lessons.", "The Penn State drill or farm out problem, rescaled to the Ekene licence and its parties."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each capstone runs its own synthetic deal, which the course never prints, and every graded figure is a return value of the engine on its fixed terms, with exactly one right answer. The Ekene deal is for rehearsal. The course uses no real deal, and the Penn State page is cited for its numbers only.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual, page OT30023 (updated 2 February 2021), turns from exploration acreage to a producing field. What form does it say the consideration for an interest in a producing field generally takes?', '["The buyer undertaking to bear future drilling costs on the field.", "A development carry recovered with simple interest from production.", "Cash or shares, the form OT30023 names.", "A royalty on the field''s output for the life of the licence."]'::jsonb, 2, 'OT30023 says the consideration for the disposal of an interest in a producing field is generally in the form of cash or shares. Bearing future drilling costs is how OT30021 describes the consideration on relatively unexplored acreage, a development carry is OT30022, and a royalty or other subordinated interest is something the engine does not compute.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'What may the holder of a marginal field do with its discovery under PIA s.94(4)(b)?', '["Farm it out, with the consent of the Commission and on terms the Commission may approve under regulations.", "Farm it out with no consent at all, since a marginal field holder is outside s.95.", "Farm it out with the Minister''s consent alone, on a percentage fee set by the Act.", "Sell it outright for cash or shares, as HMRC''s manual describes a producing field."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Section 94(4)(b) lets the holder, with the consent of the Commission and on terms and conditions as the Commission may approve under regulations, farm out the discovery; s.94(5) adds that the farmee presents a field development plan. The Act sets no percentage fee itself: s.95(12) leaves the fee to regulation. HMRC''s manual is United Kingdom guidance.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'The course quotes HM Revenue and Customs'' Oil Taxation Manual, which is United Kingdom tax guidance. What does it take from the manual?', '["The United Kingdom''s tax on a farm-out, which the engine applies to the Ekene deal as a reference case for every tier.", "Consent rules for an assignment, since the Nigerian texts leave the consent to the parties.", "The promote and the promote ratio, which the manual defines in points on its page OT30048.", "What a farm in, an earn-in, a reimbursement and a development carry are, while applying none of its tax rules."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course reads HMRC''s manual under the Open Government Licence for what a farm in, an earn-in, a reimbursement and a development carry are, and applies none of its tax rules; the engine computes no tax on the deal. Consent comes from the Act and the 2024 Regulations. The promote and its ratio are the engine''s stated convention.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Why are the earning, promote and consideration figures of this tier the stated deal arithmetic run by the engine?', '["The 2024 Regulations print a worked farm-in schedule, which the engine copies figure for figure.", "No public text prints a farm-in schedule, as the engine''s validation record says in its own words.", "The Penn State page prints the Ekene schedule.", "Licensed model agreements print the schedules, and the course copies their figures while quoting no sentence."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The validation record states: "No public text prints a farm-in schedule or a break-even promote: those goldens come from the stated deal arithmetic in the oracle." So these figures show what a set of terms produces, checked by an independent oracle. No licensed model agreement is named as a source, the Regulations are one of the public texts, and the Penn State problem is a separate cited check on another subject.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'A learner types limit inside an event''s cap object in the earning box. What does the engine''s refusal name?', '["The top-level key cap, with the eight top-level keys listed as the ones the earning call accepts.", "The path events[0].cap.limit, with on, amount and overrunRule as the accepted keys.", "Nothing: limit is read as the cap amount and the call runs.", "The field events[0].cap.amount, said to be missing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses an unknown key at whatever level it sits, with its path and the accepted keys at that level. Its words: events[0].cap.limit is not an accepted key; the accepted keys of events[0].cap are on, amount, overrunRule. It reads no key it does not know, and it names the unknown key before any missing term.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Suppose the farmor field reads XX, an id the licence (EKO 70, PA 30) does not contain. What comes back from the engine?', '["A result in which XX joins the licence at 0 percent and farms out nothing to FIN.", "A refusal on farminee.id, since the farminee must deal with a known farmor first.", "A result in which EKO, the largest holder, is taken as the farmor in place of XX.", "A refusal on farmor, naming EKO and PA as the parties it may be."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Its words: farmor must be one of "EKO", "PA"; got "XX". Only a party holding a participating interest can give one up, and the call leaves that choice to the box: no party is added at 0 percent and none is substituted. The farminee''s id has a check of its own.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'What happens when the gross cost of an event is stated as 0?', '["It refuses on events[0].grossCost, which must be a finite number above 0.", "It returns a result in which every payment, the carry and the promote are 0.000000.", "It returns a result with the promote in points and no money figures, since the well is free.", "It refuses on the promote, since there is no cost."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s words: "events[0].grossCost must be a finite number above 0; got 0". A gross cost is a stated term with its own condition, and the refusal names the field that broke it and returns no figures. The promote is set by the share paid and the interest held, and the engine never divides by the gross cost to get it.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Which texts does the basis.source of an earningObligation call cite?', '["The 2024 Regulations at reg. 19, and the Act at s.264(f) and s.302(12)(c) on the tax treatment of fees.", "Penn State EME 801, Lesson 6, and HMRC''s manual OT30021, with every chance and value a stated input.", "HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual OT30021, and the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 at s.94(8)(b) and s.95.", "The AOI Regulations at reg. 24 alone, whose definition of a petroleum agreement names farm-in agreements."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s source line for an earning call names HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual OT30021 (the work programme as consideration; a cash reimbursement of sunk costs) and the Act''s s.94(8)(b) (farm-out defined) and s.95 (every assignment needs consent). Regulation 19 and the tax sections are the consent fee''s sources, and the Penn State line belongs to the deal value call of a later tier.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'Why can a graded figure in this course have exactly one right answer?', '["Each is rounded to a whole number before it is graded, so that small differences between machines disappear.", "Each is checked against a market survey of farm-out deals, which fixes the accepted value.", "Each is taken from the Ekene Deep deal, whose figures the lessons print in full.", "Each is an engine return value on fixed inputs, and none comes from the Monte Carlo of riskSharing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course states that every graded number is a return value of the engine on fixed inputs, and that no graded figure comes from the Monte Carlo of riskSharing, so the same terms give the same number on any machine. Graded figures are quoted to six decimals, no market survey is involved, and each capstone runs its own synthetic deal, which the lessons never print.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Of a 46000000.000000 well, FIN funds 18200000.000000 and PA 13800000.000000. How much falls to EKO, the farmor, on the engine''s row?', '["14000000.000000, the farmor''s payment on the capped well.", "18200000.000000, which is the payment the engine prints for FIN on the same row.", "12000000.000000, which is EKO''s payment on the uncapped 40000000.000000 teaching well.", "13800000.000000, EKO''s 30 percent of the well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns EKO paying 14000000.000000, FIN 18200000.000000 and PA 13800000.000000, which make up the 46000000.000000 well. 12000000.000000 is EKO''s payment on a different, uncapped well of 40000000.000000. 13800000.000000 is PA''s payment, and EKO holds 40 percent after the deal.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Heads up, no cap: FIN takes 30 percent of EKO''s share by paying 30 percent of a drilled 40000000.000000 well. PA, at 30 percent, stays outside the deal. What is PA''s bill?', '["16000000.000000, EKO''s payment, since EKO holds 40 percent after the deal.", "0.000000, FIN covering PA.", "4000000.000000, PA''s carry.", "12000000.000000, PA''s share at its 30 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s other-party split returns PA paying 12000000.000000, its own 30 percent of the well, beside FIN''s 12000000.000000 and EKO''s 16000000.000000. Those three make up the 40000000.000000 well. A heads-up deal has a carry of 0.000000, and no farminee pays another party''s share.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'On an earning event with no cap, how does the share of the gross cost the farminee pays compare with the stated share paid?', '["The share of the gross cost is always the lower, by the promote in points.", "They are the same figure: with no cap the farminee pays its stated share of the whole gross cost.", "The share of the gross cost is always the higher, by the cash bonus over the gross cost.", "They differ by the reimbursement, which the engine adds to the farminee''s side of the well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With no cap the promote applies to the whole gross cost, so the engine''s column equals the stated share: 40.000000 against 40.000000 on earn-bonus-and-reimbursement, and 70.000000 on the full carry. The two part company only when a cap binds, as on the Ekene well (39.565217 against 40.000000). The bonus and the reimbursement leave the split of the well alone.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Totals count completed events only. On an undrilled deal (0 of 1 events done, "per-event"; EKO 70, PA 30) where FIN is bound to fund 40 percent of an uncapped 40000000.000000 well for 30, which farmor payment do the totals show?', '["12000000.000000 in both, since EKO pays its own share whether or not FIN drills.", "28000000.000000, EKO''s whole pre-deal share, until the event is completed.", "0.000000 in the totals, beside a row obligation of 12000000.000000 for EKO.", "16000000.000000, the farminee''s obligation, which the farmor pays until completion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only completed events enter the totals, so the farmor''s total reads 0.000000 while its row keeps the obligation of 12000000.000000. EKO''s pre-deal 70 percent of the well is no payment of any kind, and the 16000000.000000 obligation belongs to FIN.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'On relatively unexplored acreage, what does HMRC''s page OT30021 say the consideration will generally consist of?', '["Wholly or mainly the Farmer in undertaking to bear future costs, drilling for example.", "Cash paid on signing, with the drilling left to the Farmer out alone.", "Shares in the Farmer in, issued to the Farmer out at the date of the assignment.", "A reimbursement of the Farmer out''s sunk costs, in full and in cash."]'::jsonb, 0, 'OT30021 says that for relatively unexplored acreage consideration will generally consist wholly, or mainly, of the Farmer in undertaking to bear future costs (e.g. drilling). The same page adds that the Farmer in may also pay a cash reimbursement for sunk costs relating to the proportionate interest acquired. Cash or shares is OT30023, for a producing field.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'The earning rule in the engine''s basis ends with one sentence about the deal''s terms. What does that sentence say?', '["Every cap, share and interest is read from the licence file when the box leaves it out", "Every cap is set to none when it is missing, and every share to the interest earned", "Every cap, share and interest is a stated input with no default", "Every share is capped at the farmor''s interest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis ends: "Every cap, share and interest is a stated input with no default". A missing term is refused by name. The engine holds no licence file of deal terms, fills in no cap or share, and the limit on the share paid is a refusal on a stated term.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Seventy for thirty: with EKO the farmor at 70 and PA at 30, FIN funds 70 percent of an uncapped 40000000.000000 well to get 30. Which promote ratio is printed?', '["1.333333, forty for thirty.", "1.000000, heads up.", "1.724638, the promote-adjusted ratio of the Ekene Deep deal.", "2.333333, seventy paid over thirty held."]'::jsonb, 3, '70 paid over 30 held is 2.333333 (engine, earn-full-carry), with a promote of 40.000000 points. 1.333333 is forty for thirty. Heads up means paying the share earned, which the full carry does not. 1.724638 is the Ekene deal''s promote-adjusted ratio.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Step the share paid up from 30 toward 70 on an uncapped 40000000.000000 well where FIN earns 30 (EKO 70, PA 30 before). How do the promote, the ratio, the carry and the farmor''s payment move?', '["The promote, the ratio and the carry all rise, and the farmor''s payment falls to 0.000000.", "The promote rises while the carry stays at 0.000000, since the carry is set by the interest earned.", "The farmor''s payment rises with the promote, since the farmor matches the farminee''s share.", "The participating interests after the deal shift toward FIN as the share it pays rises."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns a promote of 0.000000 points and a carry of 0.000000 at 30, then 10.000000 points and 4000000.000000 at 40, up to 40.000000 points, a ratio of 2.333333 and a carry of 16000000.000000 at 70, where EKO pays 0.000000. The interests after are set by the interest earned and stay the same.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Explain the full carry''s carry of 16000000.000000: 70 paid for 30 held on a well of 40000000.000000 that has no cap.', '["It is FIN''s own 30 percent of the well plus the ten points of a standard promote.", "It is the 40 promote points applied to the well, with no cap to hold it.", "It is EKO''s pre-deal share of the well less the reimbursement FIN pays in cash.", "It is the whole of FIN''s payment for the well, which the engine counts as carry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With no cap the carry is the promote in points applied to the gross cost: 40.000000 points of 40000000.000000, which is EKO''s whole 40 percent post-deal share. By the basis rule it is also FIN''s payment of 28000000.000000 less its own 12000000.000000. The engine states no standard promote, and the deal has no reimbursement.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Can a heads-up deal carry a cash bonus?', '["No: a bonus turns the deal into a promoted one, and the engine refuses a bonus on a heads-up deal.", "No: a heads-up deal has a consideration of 0.000000 by definition, so any bonus is dropped.", "Yes: it has no promote, and a bonus is a separate stated term the engine adds to the consideration.", "Yes, but the engine counts the bonus as carry and reports a promote above 0 points."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The promote is set by the share paid and the interest held, and the cash sits beside them. On the heads-up well with a stated bonus of 2000000.000000, the engine returns a promote of 0.000000 points and a consideration of 2000000.000000. The consideration of 0.000000 on the course''s heads-up case comes from its stated bonus of 0, and the bonus is never carry.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'On a deal with a single earning event, which figure is the participating interest the farminee holds after the event?', '["The share of the gross cost it pays, since the promote vests with the payment.", "The farmor''s interest before the deal, since the whole share passes on completion.", "The interest earned less the promote in points, the part the promote pays for.", "The interest the event earns, since the farminee held none before it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'FIN holds 0.000000 before the farm-out, and on the Ekene well the engine''s held-after figure is 30.000000, the same as the interest earned. The share paid sets the payment, the farmor keeps what it does not farm out, and the promote is measured against the held-after figure.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'With no cap, what does a promote of 10 points on a 40000000.000000 well mean in money?', '["A carry of 4000000.000000 paid beyond the farminee''s own share.", "A cash bonus of 4000000.000000 paid to the farmor on signing the deal.", "A payment of 10000000.000000 for FIN''s own share.", "A reimbursement of 4000000.000000 of the farmor''s past costs on the licence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A promote in points reads straight onto money: 10 points of the gross cost beyond the farminee''s own share, which with no cap is the carry, 4000000.000000 on earn-bonus-and-reimbursement (engine). The bonus and the reimbursement are separate stated terms, and FIN''s own share of that well is 12000000.000000.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Past costs of 9000000.000000, with 30 percent of them repaid by FIN (earn-bonus-and-reimbursement): what reimbursement figure does the engine print?', '["3600000.000000, the Ekene reimbursement at the same 30 percent share.", "9000000.000000, the past costs in full.", "1500000.000000, the bonus.", "2700000.000000, 30 percent of 9000000.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Thirty percent of 9000000.000000 is the engine''s 2700000.000000. The Ekene deal repays the same share of a larger sum, 12000000.000000, giving 3600000.000000; repaying the whole would need a stated 100 percent; and the bonus of 1500000.000000 is a separate term of the same case.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'FIN earns 25 percent of sole holder EKO''s licence by paying one third (33.333333 percent at six decimals) of a drilled 12000000.000000 well; there is no cap, no bonus and no reimbursement. What consideration reaches EKO?', '["4000000.000000, the whole of FIN''s payment for the well, counted as consideration.", "0.000000, as no cash is stated.", "1000000.000000, the carry alone.", "8000000.000000, EKO''s own payment for the well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s line reads "consideration to EKO: carry 1000000 + cash bonus 0 + past-cost reimbursement 0 (0% of 0) = 1000000". With no cash the consideration is the carry, the part of EKO''s share FIN pays. FIN''s own share of the well is no consideration, and EKO''s own payment is a cost to it.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'On the Ekene deal the share reimbursed (30 percent) equals the participating interest FIN earns. Does the engine tie the two together?', '["No: the reimbursed percent is its own stated term, and the engine computes whatever share the deal states.", "Yes: it refuses any reimbursed percent that differs from the interest earned by the farminee.", "Yes: it overwrites the reimbursed percent with the interest earned, and says so in a reason line.", "No: it ignores the reimbursed percent and always reimburses the whole of the stated past costs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine reads the reimbursed percent as its own stated term: stating 50 on the Ekene past costs of 12000000.000000 returns a reimbursement of half of them. HMRC''s manual speaks of sunk costs relating to the proportionate interest acquired, and the Ekene deal states the same 30 percent, but the engine holds no rule linking the two.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Sum what FIN hands over on earn-bonus-and-reimbursement: 16000000.000000 toward the drilled well, 1500000.000000 as bonus and 2700000.000000 as reimbursement. Which total does the engine print as its outlay?', '["8200000.000000, which is the consideration to EKO on the same case, carry included.", "16000000.000000, which is FIN''s payment for the well alone, the cash left out.", "23800000.000000, which is FIN''s outlay on the Ekene Deep deal, a different well.", "20200000.000000, well payment and cash."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Adding the three stated sums gives the engine''s 20200000.000000. On this case EKO''s consideration is 8200000.000000, which counts the carry in place of FIN''s whole payment; 16000000.000000 omits the bonus and the reimbursement; and 23800000.000000 comes from a different well and different cash.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Why does the equivalent working interest sit above the share paid on an uncapped deal with a bonus and a reimbursement?', '["It divides by the vested interest, which is always below the share paid.", "It adds the cash to the well payment, so more cash moves it further above.", "It counts the carry twice, once in the payment and once as consideration.", "It uses the dry-hole cost as its gross cost, which is the smaller of the two."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The equivalent working interest is the farminee''s payment, cash bonus and reimbursement over the gross cost of the completed events: on earn-cap-gross-below, with no cash, it is 40.000000, the share paid, and on earn-bonus-and-reimbursement it is 50.500000. Dividing by the vested interest gives the promote-adjusted ratio, the carry is inside the payment only, and the gross cost is the completed events'' cost.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'What does the course say the equivalent working interest is?', '["A market value for the participating interest, which the engine reads from recent deals.", "A forecast of what another party would pay for the same interest after the farm-out.", "Arithmetic on stated terms: the heads-up share of the well the farminee''s money would buy.", "The interest that vests to the farminee once every earning event of the deal is complete."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis calls it "the heads-up interest that would cost the farminee the same", computed from the stated payment, bonus and reimbursement. The engine asserts no market value and forecasts no price; a later tier prices an interest with a separate function on a stated basis. The vested interest is a separate figure, 30.000000 on the Ekene deal against 51.739130.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Which pair of the engine''s money totals both count the cash bonus?', '["The farmor''s consideration and the farminee''s outlay.", "The farminee''s well payment and the carry, since the bonus is paid through the well.", "The farmor''s well payment and the other parties'' payments, split by participating interest.", "The promote in points and the promote ratio, which the bonus raises for the farminee."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bonus moves money from the farminee to the farmor, so the engine counts it in the consideration (beside the carry and the reimbursement) and in the outlay (beside the well payment and the reimbursement). It changes no split of the well, no party''s payment for it, and no promote. The equivalent working interest and its ratio count it too, as a share of the well.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Under reg. 4(2) of the 2024 Regulations, to whom is an application for the Minister''s consent made?', '["To the Minister directly, who then asks the Commission for its recommendation on the deal.", "To the Commission, in the manner the Regulations prescribe.", "To the farmee''s own board, which forwards it with a field development plan attached.", "To the other licence parties, whose agreement stands in."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Regulation 4(2): application for consent of the Minister under the Act and these Regulations shall be made to the Commission in the manner prescribed in these Regulations. The Minister then decides on the Commission''s recommendation (PIA s.95(2)). No text the course reads lets the parties'' agreement stand in for consent.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Under PIA s.95(4), how does a holder that wishes to transfer its interest apply?', '["To the Minister, in a format the Act sets out in a schedule to the Act itself.", "To the High Court, which approves the transfer as a matter of contract law.", "To no authority: the Act lets holders transfer by notice once the fee is paid.", "To the Commission, in the format the Commission prescribes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 95(4) says the holder shall make an application for approval of the transfer to the Commission in the format prescribed by the Commission. The Minister consents on the Commission''s recommendation, and the fee is a condition of consent and no replacement for it.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'How does reg. 24 of the 2024 Regulations define a "Petroleum Agreement" so that it reaches a farm-in?', '["It includes farm-in agreements in its list, beside production sharing contracts and joint operating agreements.", "It limits the term to production sharing contracts, so a farm-in agreement falls outside it.", "It defines the term by the value of the transaction, above the amount the Commission sets.", "It leaves farm-ins to the Act''s marginal field section, which alone defines a farm-out."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Regulation 24 says "Petroleum Agreement" includes production sharing contracts, joint operating agreements, joint venture agreements, farm-in-agreements or any other document. The value of the transaction is defined in reg. 19(3) and again in reg. 24, and the Act''s s.94(8)(b) definition sits in its marginal field section without limiting the Regulations.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Which party does limb (d) of the definition of "Assignment" in reg. 24 of the 2024 Regulations reach?', '["Only the holder of a petroleum mining lease, since the Regulations treat a prospecting licence as incapable of any assignment.", "Only a company listed on a public exchange, whose shares change hands without consent.", "A contractor or joint venture partner under an upstream petroleum arrangement, a sole risk award or a marginal field included.", "The Minister alone, when the Minister transfers a licence from one holder to another."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Limb (d) covers an assignment by a contractor or joint venture partner under an upstream petroleum arrangement, including joint venture arrangements, production sharing contracts and agreements, service contracts, a sole risk award or a marginal field. The Ekene farm-out is on a prospecting licence, which the consent rules reach, and a listed holder is the exception in reg. 3(3).'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'On what does PIA s.95(12) say the assignment fee is based?', '["The farminee''s outlay for the deal, bonus and reimbursement included.", "The consideration the farmor receives, carry included.", "A flat sum per licence.", "A percentage of the value of the transaction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Section 95(12) says the Commission shall make regulation to prescribe fees as a condition for any transaction under s.95(1), which fee shall be based on a percentage of the value of the transaction and shall not be tax deductible. The value of the transaction is a stated amount with its source, and the Professional tier computes the fee on it. The consideration and the outlay are the engine''s own totals.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The engine will not apply the gazetted fee basis to a PEL. What does its refusal tell the caller to do?', '["Restate the licence as a PPL, since every exploration licence is treated as a prospecting licence.", "State the PEL''s rates under basis \"stated\", since reg. 19(2) sets the fee for the Minister''s consent.", "Leave the fee out, since a PEL assignment needs no consent and pays no fee to anyone.", "Apply to the Minister, whose consent a PEL assignment needs in place of the Commission''s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s words: licence must be "PPL" or "PML" under basis "nuprc-2024-r19": reg. 19(2) sets the fee for the consent of the Minister, and a PEL assignment needs the consent of the Commission (reg. 16); state its rates under basis "stated"; got "PEL". A PEL''s consent is the Commission''s, and restating the licence type would misstate the deal.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'What do the earning calculator''s participating interests after the deal assume about consent?', '["That the assignment goes through: the vesting rule says nothing about consent.", "That consent was refused, so the table shows the interests the deal would have moved.", "That consent is deemed after 15 working days.", "That the fee has been paid in full."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s vesting rule reports when a participating interest vests under the deal''s terms and says nothing about consent, so the table after the deal assumes the assignment goes through. A farm-out signed but not consented has moved no participating interest. The engine computes no consent, counts no working days and checks no payment in the earning call.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Cash aside, earn-bonus-and-reimbursement moves 30 percent from EKO (70 before) to FIN; PA holds 30; the well is drilled under "per-event". Which shares follow?', '["EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 30.000000, less the reimbursed share of EKO''s past costs.", "Unchanged at EKO 70.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 0.000000, until the bonus has been paid over in full.", "A 40, 30, 30 table for EKO, PA and FIN: the cash moves no share.", "EKO 20.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 50.500000, taking the equivalent interest as the vested one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine returns EKO 40.000000, PA 30.000000 and FIN 30.000000: the interest earned moves from the farmor, and the bonus and the reimbursement change what FIN pays for it. The equivalent working interest of 50.500000 is a measure of price, and no text or term the engine reads makes the interest wait on the bonus.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'What vested participating interest does the engine return on the Ekene Deep deal, one event completed under "per-event"?', '["40.000000, the share paid.", "51.739130, the equivalent working interest of FIN''s outlay on the deal.", "0.000000 until consent.", "30.000000, the interest the one event earns."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each completed event vests its stated interest, and the engine returns a vested participating interest of 30.000000. 40.000000 is the share paid and 51.739130 the equivalent working interest, both measures of price. The engine computes no consent, so vesting follows the deal''s terms.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'What is the first line of the course''s checklist for reading a farm-out, and what check goes with it?', '["The licence and its parties with their participating interests before the deal, which must sum to 100.", "The consideration to the farmor, which must equal the value of the transaction on the application.", "The promote in points, which must lie between 0 and the farmor''s interest before the deal.", "The consent needed, which must be granted before any other line of the checklist is read."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The checklist opens with the licence and its parties and their participating interests before the deal, and the engine refuses shares that do not sum to 100. The consideration and the value of the transaction are kept apart, the promote is read from the event line, and consent is line 8, read from the texts.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Line 9 of the checklist asks for every source applied, with its edition and the date read. Where does a learner find the sources the engine applied?', '["In the reasons, whose last line lists each text the engine read for the call.", "In the Source block, which prints the engine''s basis.source for the call.", "In the start selector, which names the source text of each worked case it loads.", "In the refusal message, which cites a text for every term it checks."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every result carries a basis block with its source, and the panel prints it in the Source block; for an earning call it names HMRC''s OT30021 and the Act''s s.94(8)(b) and s.95. The course''s sources table gives each text''s edition and the date it was read, 2026-09-27 for all. The reasons explain figures, and a refusal names a field and its condition.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'At which levels of a box does the engine refuse a key it does not read?', '["At the top level only, since an event''s keys are read by the engine as free text and never checked.", "Only inside a cap object, since a misspelt cap is the one mistake that moves money.", "At whatever level the key sits, naming its path and the full list of accepted keys there.", "At no level: an unknown key is dropped quietly and the rest of the box runs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course states that an input key a function does not read is refused at whatever level it sits (a top-level option, an event, a cap, a project, a signal, a holding), with the path to the key and the full list of accepted keys. A misspelt optional key is refused so that no term is silently dropped.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The full carry case has FIN paying 70 percent of a completed 40000000.000000 well with no cap for 30 percent, with no bonus and no past costs. What equivalent working interest does the engine return?', '["30.000000, which is the participating interest that FIN earns and vests on this deal.", "2.333333, which is the promote ratio of seventy paid for thirty on the same well.", "40.000000, which is the promote in points of the same deal, read as a percentage.", "70.000000, the share of the well paid."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With no cash the outlay is the well payment, 28000000.000000, which over the 40000000.000000 well is 70.000000 percent (engine). The interest earned is 30.000000, the promote ratio is 2.333333 and the promote is 40.000000 points: each is a different figure from the heads-up share the outlay would buy.'),
  ('farmout', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'In what currency and unit does the Ekene farm-out fixture state its money?', '["Nigerian naira, converted at a rate stated in the fixture''s header for each year.", "US dollars, in whole dollars.", "US dollars in thousands.", "The currency each party reports in, which the engine converts to a common unit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s fixture section says money is in US$, whole dollars, and the fixture file names US$ as its currency. No exchange rate appears in the file, and the figures are used as stated, with no scaling. The engine converts nothing.');
